# <%= current_page.data.title %>

Envoy Gateway uses the [Kubernetes Gateway API](https://gateway-api.sigs.k8s.io/)
to route HTTP and HTTPS requests to your application. Cloud Platform manages
the Gateway, its load balancer and its default TLS certificate. Your team
creates an `HTTPRoute` in your application's namespace to connect a hostname
and path to a Kubernetes Service.

This guide covers shared Gateways with Coraza enabled.

The platform's `default` Gateway has an HTTP listener on port 80 and its
`default-listenerset` has an HTTPS listener on port 443.

## Choose a shared ListenerSet

For a platform domain covered by a shared Gateway certificate, set
`parentRefs` on your HTTPRoute to the ListenerSet for the Gateway you need.
Cloud Platform manages these ListenerSets; you do not need to create your own.
The following are available on the `live` cluster:

| Gateway | ListenerSet | Access |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `default` | `default-listenerset` | Public |
| `default-non-prod` | `default-non-prod-listenerset` | Public, non-production |
| `internal` | `internal-listenerset` | Internal |
| `internal-non-prod` | `internal-non-prod-listenerset` | Internal, non-production |

Use the ListenerSet name in `parentRefs.name`, with
`namespace: envoy-gateway-system`, `kind: ListenerSet` and
`sectionName: https`. Check that the selected Gateway is available in your
cluster and that its certificate covers your hostname.

## Route HTTPS traffic to your application

Create an `HTTPRoute` in your namespace. Replace the
namespace, hostname, Service name and Service **port** with your own values.
The port is the Service's `spec.ports[].port`, not necessarily the container's
port. The hostname must be covered by the Gateway certificate. This example
uses the public `default-listenerset`; select the appropriate shared
ListenerSet from the table above for your application.

```yaml
apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: HTTPRoute
metadata:
  name: my-app
  namespace: my-namespace
spec:
  parentRefs:
    - group: gateway.networking.k8s.io
      kind: ListenerSet
      name: default-listenerset
      namespace: envoy-gateway-system
      sectionName: https
  hostnames:
    - my-app.example.com
  rules:
    - matches:
        - path:
            type: PathPrefix
            value: /
      backendRefs:
        - name: my-app-service
          port: 8080
```

Save this as `httproute.yaml`, apply it using your normal deployment process,
and check its status:

```sh
kubectl apply -f httproute.yaml
kubectl describe httproute my-app -n my-namespace
kubectl get gateway default -n envoy-gateway-system -o yaml
```

Check the route's parent status for `Accepted=True` and `ResolvedRefs=True`.
Also check that the Gateway is programmed and has an address. The Service must
have ready endpoints:

```sh
kubectl get endpointslice -n my-namespace \
  -l kubernetes.io/service-name=my-app-service
```

For eligible platform-managed domains, ExternalDNS can create DNS records
from HTTPRoutes. This depends on the controller, domain filters and DNS zone
configuration; an accepted route does not by itself guarantee DNS is ready.
For a new hostname, confirm the record points to the intended Gateway and test
`https://my-app.example.com/`. Check the certificate in your browser or with
`curl -v`. Do not publish a route using an existing production hostname until
you have planned the DNS cutover described below.

## Use an internal Gateway

Use the `internal` Gateway for internal applications and
`internal-non-prod` for internal non-production applications. These Gateways
use private network load balancers, like the corresponding internal NGINX
Ingress classes. Clients need network connectivity to the load balancer,
such as through an approved connected network, and any relevant security
rules must allow the traffic. An internal load balancer does not provide
application authentication.

On `live`, the shared certificates cover
`*.internal.cloud-platform.service.justice.gov.uk` for `internal` and
`*.internal-non-prod.cloud-platform.service.justice.gov.uk` for
`internal-non-prod`. A wildcard covers one DNS label, for example
`my-app.internal.cloud-platform.service.justice.gov.uk`, not
`api.my-app.internal.cloud-platform.service.justice.gov.uk`.

Create an HTTPRoute in your application namespace. For example, the following
uses the internal non-production ListenerSet:

```yaml
apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: HTTPRoute
metadata:
  name: my-app-internal
  namespace: my-namespace
spec:
  parentRefs:
    - group: gateway.networking.k8s.io
      kind: ListenerSet
      name: internal-non-prod-listenerset
      namespace: envoy-gateway-system
      sectionName: https
  hostnames:
    - my-app.internal-non-prod.cloud-platform.service.justice.gov.uk
  rules:
    - backendRefs:
        - name: my-app-service
          port: 8080
```

Replace the namespace, hostname, Service and Service port with your own
values. For the `internal` Gateway, use `internal-listenerset` and an
appropriate internal hostname instead. Check the HTTPRoute is accepted and
has resolved references as described above; your application's NetworkPolicy
must also allow traffic from the selected Envoy proxy to the backend.

ExternalDNS can publish eligible hostnames in the platform's internal zones,
but DNS publication is subject to its filters and does not guarantee network
access. For example, the current exclusion for test-cluster domains also
matches some application hostnames beginning with `cp-` or `yy-`. Confirm DNS
and test HTTPS from an authorised network. Coordinate with Cloud Platform
before using an existing Ingress hostname to avoid DNS conflicts.

## Use a custom domain

For a hostname not covered by the platform certificate, use your own
certificate and `ListenerSet`, then attach an `HTTPRoute` to that listener.
This follows the same pattern as the Container Platform, but uses an explicit
cert-manager `Certificate`.

Follow the [custom domain guide](custom-domain-cert.html#setup) to set up and
delegate a DNS zone and create a `Certificate` in your application namespace.
Confirm the certificate is ready before configuring the listener. The current
Let's Encrypt issuer uses DNS-01 validation and needs access to a suitable
Route 53 zone for the challenge record.

Save the following resources in your namespace folder in the
[environments repository](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/cloud-platform-environments/).
Replace the namespace, hostname, Certificate Secret name, Service name and
Service port with your own values:

```yaml
apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: ListenerSet
metadata:
  name: my-app-https
  namespace: <my-namespace>
spec:
  parentRef:
    group: gateway.networking.k8s.io
    kind: Gateway
    name: default
    namespace: envoy-gateway-system
  listeners:
    - name: https
      hostname: <hostname>
      protocol: HTTPS
      port: 443
      tls:
        mode: Terminate
        certificateRefs:
          - group: ""
            kind: Secret
            name: <my-cert-secret>
      allowedRoutes:
        namespaces:
          from: Same
        kinds:
          - group: gateway.networking.k8s.io
            kind: HTTPRoute
---
apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: HTTPRoute
metadata:
  name: my-app
  namespace: <my-namespace>
spec:
  parentRefs:
    - group: gateway.networking.k8s.io
      kind: ListenerSet
      name: my-app-https
      sectionName: https
  hostnames:
    - <hostname>
  rules:
    - backendRefs:
        - name: <my-svc>
          port: 80
```

The Secret must be in the same namespace as the `ListenerSet`, and
`<my-cert-secret>` must match the `Certificate`'s `secretName`. Use the
Service's port, not necessarily its container port. This example serves HTTPS
only; it does not configure an HTTP-to-HTTPS redirect.

Check that the listener is accepted and programmed, its references are
resolved, and the route is accepted with resolved references:

```sh
kubectl describe certificate <my-cert> -n <my-namespace>
kubectl describe listenerset my-app-https -n <my-namespace>
kubectl describe httproute my-app -n <my-namespace>
```

The listener can report `OverlappingTLSConfig` because the platform's default
HTTPS listener accepts all hostnames. The tested custom listener was accepted
and served its own certificate despite this condition; check its status and
contact the platform team if you have concerns.

ExternalDNS v2 can publish the `HTTPRoute` hostname when it is permitted to
manage the DNS zone. Confirm the record points to the intended Envoy load
balancer and verify HTTPS with the custom hostname. If another provider manages
the zone, coordinate the traffic DNS record with its owner.

**Do not create a route or change DNS for an existing production hostname
without a migration plan.** Ingress and HTTPRoute records may be managed by
different ExternalDNS controllers and must be coordinated to avoid conflicts
or interrupted traffic. Contact `#ask-cloud-platform` before migrating.

## Coraza web application firewall

Coraza is a web application firewall (WAF) that inspects HTTP traffic using
the [OWASP Core Rule Set](https://coreruleset.org/). The Cloud Platform Gateway
module enables Coraza by default and configures `SecRuleEngine On` with the
Core Rule Set and platform-managed rules. This is different from the opt-in
ModSecurity annotations on NGINX Ingress. A WAF does not replace application
authentication, validation or other security controls.

An individual Gateway can be configured without Coraza. Confirm that the
Gateway you use has the policy enabled and that your route is protected before
relying on it. The team managing the Gateway can check:

```sh
kubectl get envoyextensionpolicy -n envoy-gateway-system
kubectl describe envoyextensionpolicy default-coraza-waf -n envoy-gateway-system
```

### Coraza modes

Coraza has three rule engine modes:

| Mode | Behaviour |
| --- | --- |
| `On` | Inspects requests and blocks those that trigger a blocking rule. This is the default for a Gateway with Coraza enabled. |
| `DetectionOnly` | Inspects requests and logs rule matches but does not block them. Use this to investigate false positives or test rules. |
| `Off` | Disables Coraza rule processing for the policy. Requests are not protected by its WAF rules. |

An application team can override the Gateway policy for an individual
HTTPRoute by creating an `EnvoyExtensionPolicy` in the same namespace as the
route, subject to permissions and any platform policies. A route-level policy
**replaces** the Gateway policy for that route; it does not merge with it.
Contact Cloud Platform before reducing protection, and test changes in
non-production.

#### Use detection-only mode

For example, to inspect traffic to the `my-app` HTTPRoute without blocking it,
create the following policy in the route's namespace:

```yaml
apiVersion: gateway.envoyproxy.io/v1alpha1
kind: EnvoyExtensionPolicy
metadata:
  name: my-app-coraza-detection
  namespace: my-namespace
spec:
  targetRefs:
    - group: gateway.networking.k8s.io
      kind: HTTPRoute
      name: my-app
  dynamicModule:
    - name: composer
      filterName: coraza-waf
      config:
        directives:
          - Include @coraza.conf
          - SecRuleEngine DetectionOnly
          - Include @crs-setup.conf
          - Include @owasp_crs/*.conf
          - Include /etc/coraza/cp/coraza-cp.conf
```

Replace `my-namespace` and `my-app`, then apply the policy and check that it
is accepted:

```sh
kubectl apply -f my-app-coraza-detection.yaml
kubectl describe envoyextensionpolicy my-app-coraza-detection -n my-namespace
```

The policy must include the complete current platform baseline, because it
replaces the Gateway policy. Confirm the baseline with Cloud Platform and
maintain it when the platform configuration changes. Only one
`EnvoyExtensionPolicy` can target a given route; update an existing policy
instead of creating a second one.

Test a normal request and a safe request that would trigger a WAF rule.
In detection-only mode the WAF should not block the request; ask Cloud
Platform to check the proxy logs for the rule match. Other components may
still reject a request. To restore blocking, change the existing policy to
`SecRuleEngine On` and apply it, or remove the route policy to inherit the
Gateway policy again. Confirm the resulting policy and test it before relying
on protection.

### Add rules for a route

To customise rules, create an `EnvoyExtensionPolicy` in the **same namespace**
as the `HTTPRoute`. This requires permission to create the policy and a
Gateway with the Coraza dynamic module configured. Check with Cloud Platform
before applying custom rules, and test in non-production first.

**A route-level policy takes precedence over the Gateway policy; it is not
just an addition to it.** Include the complete current baseline configuration
as well as your custom rules, or you could unintentionally remove WAF
protection. The baseline below reflects the platform Gateway module; confirm
it is still current with Cloud Platform and maintain it when the platform
configuration changes. Only one `EnvoyExtensionPolicy` can target a given
route.

For example, the following adds a rule that blocks requests whose path starts
with `/example-blocked-path`. Use your own rule ID and condition, and ensure
the ID does not collide with another rule:

```yaml
apiVersion: gateway.envoyproxy.io/v1alpha1
kind: EnvoyExtensionPolicy
metadata:
  name: my-app-coraza
  namespace: my-namespace
spec:
  targetRefs:
    - group: gateway.networking.k8s.io
      kind: HTTPRoute
      name: my-app
  dynamicModule:
    - name: composer
      filterName: coraza-waf
      config:
        directives:
          - Include @coraza.conf
          - SecRuleEngine On
          - Include @crs-setup.conf
          - Include @owasp_crs/*.conf
          - Include /etc/coraza/cp/coraza-cp.conf
          - >-
            SecRule REQUEST_URI "@beginsWith /example-blocked-path"
            "id:1001000,phase:1,deny,status:403,log,msg:'Example blocked path'"
```

Apply the policy using your deployment process and check its status:

```sh
kubectl apply -f my-app-coraza.yaml
kubectl describe envoyextensionpolicy my-app-coraza -n my-namespace
```

Check that it is accepted, then test both a normal request and a request to
the example blocked path. The latter should return HTTP 403. A 403 alone does
not prove which component blocked the request; check the available WAF logs
with Cloud Platform if needed. Avoid including real credentials or sensitive
request data in test requests or support messages.

For false positives, share the affected hostname, time, request ID if
available, and rule ID with Cloud Platform. Prefer a narrowly scoped exception
over disabling the WAF or an entire rule set. Do not edit the
platform-managed Coraza ConfigMap: it is managed centrally.

## Migrate from NGINX Ingress

You can move traffic gradually from NGINX to Envoy using Route 53 weighted
records. The Ingress and HTTPRoute use the same hostname and Service, but
different DNS set identifiers.

First, test with a separate non-production hostname. Check the selected
Gateway, certificate, backend NetworkPolicy and Coraza behaviour. 

### Example: start with NGINX at 100 and Envoy at 0

The following examples use the public `default` Gateway. Replace the
namespace, hostname, Service and Service port with your values. 

Configure the existing Ingress with weight `100`:

```yaml
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: my-app
  namespace: my-namespace
  annotations:
    external-dns.alpha.kubernetes.io/set-identifier: my-app-my-namespace-green
    external-dns.alpha.kubernetes.io/aws-weight: "100"
spec:
  ingressClassName: default
  rules:
    - host: my-app.apps.live.cloud-platform.service.justice.gov.uk
      http:
        paths:
          - path: /
            pathType: Prefix
            backend:
              service:
                name: my-app-service
                port:
                  number: 80
```

Once the Ingress weighted record is confirmed in Route 53, add the HTTPRoute
with weight `0`:

```yaml
apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: HTTPRoute
metadata:
  name: my-app
  namespace: my-namespace
  annotations:
    external-dns.kubernetes.io/set-identifier: my-app-my-namespace-envoy
    external-dns.kubernetes.io/aws-weight: "0"
spec:
  parentRefs:
    - group: gateway.networking.k8s.io
      kind: ListenerSet
      name: default-listenerset
      namespace: envoy-gateway-system
      sectionName: https
  hostnames:
    - my-app.apps.live.cloud-platform.service.justice.gov.uk
  rules:
    - matches:
        - path:
            type: PathPrefix
            value: /
      backendRefs:
        - name: my-app-service
          port: 80
```

The annotation prefixes are intentionally different: use
`external-dns.alpha.kubernetes.io` for Ingress and
`external-dns.kubernetes.io` for HTTPRoute. Preserve any other settings needed
by your existing Ingress; these examples do not reproduce application-specific
configuration.

Confirm both weighted records point to the intended load balancers and that
the HTTPRoute has `Accepted=True` and `ResolvedRefs=True`. Test the Envoy
load balancer directly, preserving the hostname for TLS and routing:

```sh
curl -v --connect-to my-app.apps.live.cloud-platform.service.justice.gov.uk:443:<envoy-load-balancer-dns>:443 \
  https://my-app.apps.live.cloud-platform.service.justice.gov.uk/
```

Use the Envoy load balancer DNS name and do not use `-k` to bypass certificate
checks. Verify application behaviour, authentication and WAF rules before
sending traffic to Envoy.

### Shift traffic and monitor

Update the two weight annotations through your normal deployment process:

| Stage | Ingress weight | HTTPRoute weight |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Initial | 100 | 0 |
| Split | 50 | 50 |
| Complete | 0 | 100 |

Wait for ExternalDNS to reconcile and verify the actual Route 53 records after
each update before proceeding. Weights control the relative share of DNS
answers, not an exact percentage of requests. DNS caching and existing
connections mean changes and rollback are not immediate. A zero-weight record
is not an absolute traffic block: Route 53 may return it if other records are
unhealthy or all weights are zero. Do not set both records to zero.

Monitor application errors and WAF blocks. If needed, restore the Ingress
weight to `100`, confirm it has reconciled, then set the HTTPRoute weight to
`0` and verify both records.
Keep the Ingress until the migration is validated and the rollback window has
ended. Remove it through your normal deployment process and confirm the
remaining DNS records are managed as intended.